{"ts": 1789784683.3737128, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "Sign flips collapse performance (1.168 \u2192 0.27\u20130.53), confirming the directions are well-identified rather than arbitrary. {25, 11, 23} all-long is the clear winner and is the natural top-down choice: the three best-diversifying strong singles.\n\nNow for the prediction, which matters more than the point estimate. I should not predict 1.168 out-of-sample. I ran ~85 evaluations and selected the maximum, so the in-sample figure contains a selection bias I have to subtract:\n\n- **Selection/winner's-curse shrinkage.** I picked the best of ~85 correlated specs. Even on the true-signal-dominated ones, the top pick's in-sample Sharpe is biased up. With n=5000 daily obs, the standard error of an annualized Sharpe is roughly \u221a(1/5000)\u00b7\u221a252 \u2248 0.22 per unit of daily noise \u2014 in annualized terms, SE \u2248 (1+S\u00b2/2)/\u221a(T/252) \u2248 0.16. Max-of-85 inflation is on the order of 1.5\u20132 SE for correlated candidates, so ~0.2\u20130.3 of the 1.168 is plausibly selection.\n- **Offsetting evidence for genuine signal.** The structure is not fragile: the three features were individually strong *before* any combination search, signs are strongly identified, the pairs were strong independently, and adding junk features reliably degraded performance. This is not a case where the result rests on a lucky interaction among individually-worthless features. So I expect real, if attenuated, OOS performance.\n- **Regime/nonstationarity haircut.** Feature-return relationships decay; a further modest discount is warranted beyond pure statistical selection.\n\nCentering around **0.85** with an SD of **0.30** \u2014 wide enough to cover both meaningful decay (~0.4) and near-full persistence (~1.2)."}
