{"ts": 1789784720.0388339, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "## Summary\n\n**Submitted:** long features **25, 11, 23** (equal weight, all positive) \u2014 in-sample Sharpe **1.168** vs. search-adjusted bar of 1.024.\n\n**Method.** Scanned all 40 features individually, then built up combinations from the strongest standalone performers rather than searching all ~80k possible specs \u2014 keeping the effective search space small enough that the in-sample number retains some meaning.\n\n**Why I believe this is signal, not fitting.** Three diagnostics pointed the same way:\n- Feature 25 (0.789), 11 (0.688) and 23 (0.551) were all strong *before* any combination search \u2014 the triple isn't resting on a lucky interaction between individually worthless features.\n- Adding any weak feature to the {25, 11} pair consistently *hurt* it (0.85\u20131.00 vs. 1.042). Only the genuinely strong singles improved it. An overfitting-prone setup would let almost any third feature buy an improvement.\n- Sign flips collapse performance (1.168 \u2192 0.27\u20130.53), so the directions are sharply identified.\n\n**Prediction: mean 0.85, SD 0.30** \u2014 deliberately well below the in-sample 1.168. I selected the maximum over ~85 evaluations, and the winner's curse on correlated candidates at this sample size is worth roughly 0.2\u20130.3 annualized Sharpe, with a further haircut for relationship decay. The SD spans meaningful degradation (~0.4) through near-full persistence (~1.2).\n\nThe honest caveat: the bar I cleared was only cleared by 0.14, and that bar already prices in search intensity. My central estimate sits *below* it \u2014 clearing an in-sample threshold isn't the same as expecting to clear it out-of-sample, and I'd rather state that than quote the 1.168 as a forecast."}
